/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Processing Processing
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Zero-copy signal-memory bindings and processor contracts.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Model Ownership and processing model
 * DspCore separates signal memory from signal processing:
 *
 * @code
 * Host, JUCE callback, or future SignalGraph
 *     owns sample memory and SignalBlock objects
 *                 |
 *                 v
 * Input SignalManager  --->  ProcessorBase  --->  Output SignalManager
 *                 |                                  |
 *                 v                                  v
 *        SignalBlock<const T>                  SignalBlock<T>
 * @endcode
 *
 * A SignalBlock is a small, non-owning view over externally owned contiguous
 * samples. It never allocates, copies, frees, queues, resamples, or schedules
 * samples. SignalManager is likewise non-owning: it stores ordered pointers to
 * blocks for one side of a process call. The host must keep the blocks and
 * their sample memory alive for that call. The manager also does not own the
 * small pointer array used to bind the blocks.
 *
 * A ProcessorBase owns no external input or output block. It may own internal
 * algorithm state: FIR owns taps and delay history, IIR owns numerator,
 * denominator, and delay histories. This makes an in-place JUCE callback or a
 * microcontroller DMA buffer usable without an intermediate copy.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Typed Typed blocks and heterogeneous managers
 * Construct an input block from read-only memory and output storage from
 * writable memory:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * const float audio[] = { 1.0F, 0.0F };
 * short control[] = { 2, -1 };
 * float result[2]{};
 *
 * auto audioBlock = MakeInputSignalBlock(audio, 2U);
 * auto controlBlock = MakeInputSignalBlock(control, 2U);
 * auto resultBlock = MakeOutputSignalBlock(result, 2U);
 * @endcode
 * `SignalBlock<const float>` and `SignalBlock<short>` may appear in one input
 * manager. A processor asks for its expected type at a named port. The manager
 * validates that request internally; users do not assign type IDs, register
 * types, or maintain a list of supported sample formats. Any user-defined T
 * can be represented by SignalBlock<T>. A particular DSP processor can still
 * impose its own requirements; FIR and IIR require arithmetic sample types.
 *
 * SignalBlockBase is the private type-erasure seam that makes the collection
 * possible. It is visible only because a host creates the ordered pointer
 * array passed to SignalManager. Application and processor code should create
 * and use SignalBlock<T>; it must never cast a SignalBlockBase pointer. The
 * manager performs the safe checked conversion after Read<T> or Write<T>.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_PortOrder Port order and named enums
 * The manager preserves block order. A processor gives that order meaning with
 * enums rather than magic numeric literals:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * class GainAndOffset final : public ProcessorBase {
 * public:
 *     enum Input : cpstd::size_t { Audio = 0U, Offset = 1U };
 *     enum Output : cpstd::size_t { ProcessedAudio = 0U };
 *
 *     bool process(const SignalManager& inputs, SignalManager& outputs) noexcept override {
 *         const auto* audio = inputs.Read<float>(Audio);
 *         const auto* offset = inputs.Read<short>(Offset);
 *         auto* result = outputs.Write<float>(ProcessedAudio);
 *         if (audio == nullptr || offset == nullptr || result == nullptr ||
 *             audio->Size() != offset->Size() || result->Capacity() < audio->Size()) {
 *             return false;
 *         }
 *         for (cpstd::size_t i = 0U; i < audio->Size(); ++i) {
 *             result->Data()[i] = audio->Data()[i] + static_cast<float>(offset->Data()[i]);
 *         }
 *         return outputs.Commit(ProcessedAudio, audio->Size());
 *     }
 *     void reset() noexcept override {}
 * };
 * @endcode
 *
 * Inputs contain their valid Size at binding time. Outputs begin with Size()==0
 * and expose Capacity. A successful processor calls SignalManager::Commit to
 * record how many output samples it produced. Commit rejects sizes greater than
 * Capacity and preserves the earlier output size on failure.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Access Access and failure rules
 * Read<T> succeeds only for an in-range read-only SignalBlock<const T>.
 * Write<T> succeeds only for an in-range writable SignalBlock<T>. A mismatched
 * type, access direction, invalid index, or invalid bound block returns
 * `nullptr`; no conversion, allocation, or implicit buffer is created. A
 * processor should validate the resulting pointers and its expected manager
 * counts before writing. This lets the host retain ownership while the
 * processor makes all algorithm-specific topology requirements explicit.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_WhatThisIsNot What processing does not provide
 * SignalManager is not a graph owner, stream clock, sample-rate converter,
 * thread synchronizer, or FIFO. Within a synchronous audio callback, managers
 * bind caller-owned blocks directly for zero-copy processing. A future graph
 * or runtime layer may add queues and resamplers only at an actual asynchronous
 * or differing-sample-rate boundary.
 */

/**
 * @namespace DspCore::Processing
 * @ingroup DspCore_Processing
 * @brief Non-owning blocks, ordered managers, and processor contracts.
 */
